title: how
pid: c81daf4d-eb18-451e-9db2-62f68fa0cd1e
body: |2-
  # PITFALL: There are two distinct kinds of `prune` steps, both necessary.
  # See their comments for why.

  remote=origin         # or something
  branch=research # or something

  # The simple case: no worktrees.

  git push $remote -d $branch # Delete $branch on the remote.
  git branch       -d $branch
  git remote prune $remote    # Drop $branch from *local* record of remote.

  # DANGER: Read these comments.
  # If there's a worktree with submodules,
  # and the branch is in a worktree in this folder,
  # *with the same name as the branch*, the following works.
  # (Without submodules it could be simpler, but whatever.)

  git push $remote -d $branch # Deletes $branch on the remote.
  rm -rf $branch              # DANGER! The worktree must have the same name.
  git worktree prune # Clear the stale worktree entry in .bare/worktrees/.
                     # Without this, 'git branch -d' refuses
                     # with "cannot delete branch used by worktree
  git branch -d $branch       # -d works since it's fully merged; no -D needed.
  git remote prune $remote    # Drops $branch from *local* record of remote.
contains:
- c43f95a8-c6f1-446c-84e2-285baad1c0ff
